**Steps to reproduce or a small repository showing the problem:**
Seems similar to #3213 , but I'm seeing this in postgres instead of mysql.
When I query an object that contains @CreateDateColumn fields, the value of those fields comes back 5 hours ahead of the current time. I am in a -5 timezone, so it does seem like it's assuming the value I get back from Postgres is in my local time instead of UTC, and then adding 5 hours to compensate.

I have a simple entity (Test) containing @CreateDateColumn and @UpdateDateColumn fields. I've also added a manually-set timestamp field, `date`, to make the problem more apparent.

```ts
import {
  Entity,
  CreateDateColumn,
  UpdateDateColumn,
  Column,
  PrimaryColumn,
  PrimaryGeneratedColumn
} from "typeorm";

@Entity()
export class Test {
  @PrimaryGeneratedColumn()
  id: number;

  @CreateDateColumn()
  created: Date;

  @UpdateDateColumn()
  updated: Date;

  @Column()
  date: Date;
}
```
Now I create a Test entity, set its date field, and retrieve it

```ts
    console.log(new Date().toISOString());
    let entity = new Test();
    entity.date = new Date();
    await this.connection.getRepository(Test).save(entity);
    let fetched = await this.connection.getRepository(Test).findOne(2);
    console.log(fetched);
```
The resulting output is
```
Test {
  id: 3,
  created: 2018-12-19T19:05:32.054Z,
  updated: 2018-12-19T19:05:32.054Z,
  date: 2018-12-19T14:05:32.040Z
}
```
 
So to walk through the results, the current time UTC is 2018-12-19T14:05:32.040Z (2:05pm UTC/9:05am EST).
The *date* field is set to the current time, and the resulting objects comes back correctly as 2:05pm UTC/9:05am EST. The created/updated fields, however, both show 7:05pm UTC/2:05pm EST. So they're 5 hours ahead of the actual time.

I would have expected the created, updated, and date fields to all have close to the same time.
